(>> 1.1 Structural Foundations: Prokaryotic vs. Eukaryotic Cells) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Cell biology forms the foundational paradigm of biotechnology. Living organisms are) Tj
T*
(broadly categorized based on their ultrastructural organization into Prokaryotes) Tj
T*
(\(domains Bacteria and Archaea\) and Eukaryotes \(animals, plants, fungi, and protists\).) Tj
T*
(* Prokaryotic Architecture: Prokaryotes lack membrane-delimited intracellular) Tj
T*
(organelles. Their genetic material consists of a single, covalently closed circular) Tj
T*
(double-stranded DNA molecule situated in an irregularly shaped cytoplasmic region) Tj
T*
(known as the nucleoid. They possess 70S ribosomes \(composed of a 50S large subunit) Tj
T*
(and a 30S small subunit\). The cell envelope comprises the plasma membrane, a) Tj
T*
(peptidoglycan cell wall \(thick in Gram-positive bacteria, thin with an outer) Tj
T*
(lipopolysaccharide membrane in Gram-negative bacteria\), and occasionally an outer) Tj
T*
(glycocalyx capsule.) Tj
T*
(* Eukaryotic Compartmentalization: Eukaryotes possess a defined double-membrane) Tj
T*
(nucleus containing linear chromosomes compacted with basic histone proteins into) Tj
T*
(chromatin fibers. Compartmentalization enables distinct biochemical reactions to) Tj
T*
(proceed concurrently under tailored microenvironmental conditions \(e.g., acidic pH in) Tj
T*
(lysosomes vs. alkaline pH in the mitochondrial matrix\). Cytoplasm contains 80S) Tj
T*
(ribosomes \(60S and 40S subunits\) and an extensive endomembrane system.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
