T*
(* Simple Diffusion: Passive flux of small, non-polar molecules \(O2, CO2, N2, steroid) Tj
T*
(hormones\) driven exclusively by concentration gradients according to Fick's First) Tj
T*
(Law: J = -P * \(C2 - C1\).) Tj
T*
(* Facilitated Diffusion: Protein-mediated passive transport exhibiting saturation) Tj
T*
(kinetics following Michaelis-Menten principles. Utilizes carrier proteins \(GLUT) Tj
T*
(uniporters undergoing conformational shifts\) and ion channels \(voltage-gated,) Tj
T*
(ligand-gated, mechanosensitive\) which transport ions at rates approaching 10^7) Tj
T*
(ions/sec.) Tj
T*
(* Primary Active Transport: Directly couples ATP hydrolysis to solute translocation) Tj
T*
(against electrochemical gradients. Exemplified by the Na+/K+ ATPase, an electrogenic) Tj
T*
(P-type pump that hydrolyzes 1 ATP to export 3 Na+ ions and import 2 K+ ions,) Tj
T*
(establishing cellular resting membrane potential \(-70 mV\) and maintaining cell) Tj
T*
(volume.) Tj
T*
(* Secondary Active Transport: Utilizes the potential energy stored in an) Tj
T*
(electrochemical gradient created by primary pumps to drive transport of another) Tj
T*
(solute against its gradient. Symport \(co-transport\) moves both solutes in the same) Tj
T*
(direction \(e.g., SGLT1 Na+/glucose transporter in intestinal enterocytes\). Antiport) Tj
T*
(\(counter-transport\) moves solutes in opposite directions \(e.g., Na+/H+ exchanger,) Tj
T*
(Na+/Ca2+ exchanger\).) Tj
T*
(* Bulk Transport: Endocytosis \(cellular uptake via vesicular budding\) subdivided into) Tj
T*
(Phagocytosis \('cell eating' of large particulates by specialized macrophages\),) Tj
T*
(Pinocytosis \('cell drinking' of fluid phase solutes\), and Receptor-Mediated) Tj
T*
(Endocytosis \(clathrin-coated pits capturing LDL via LDL receptors\). Exocytosis) Tj
T*
(mediates targeted secretory vesicle fusion via SNARE proteins.) Tj
