([EXAMPLE 2: Michaelis-Menten Kinetics of Glucose Transporter \(GLUT1\)]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Context: Erythrocyte glucose uptake is mediated by the facilitated uniporter) Tj
T*
(GLUT1. If Km for D-glucose is 1.5 mM and Vmax is 30 micromol/min/10^8 cells, calculate) Tj
T*
(the transport flux \(V\) at physiological fasting blood glucose \(5.0 mM\) and severe) Tj
T*
(hypoglycemic condition \(1.0 mM\). Compare with D-galactose \(Km = 30 mM\).) Tj
T*
(Formula: Michaelis-Menten hyperbolic saturation relation: V = \(Vmax * [S]\) / \(Km + [S]\).) Tj
T*
(Calculation 1 \(Physiological Fasting Glucose, [S] = 5.0 mM\):) Tj
T*
(V_fasting = \(30 * 5.0\) / \(1.5 + 5.0\) = 150 / 6.5 = 23.08 micromol/min/10^8 cells.) Tj
T*
(Operating saturation percentage = 23.08 / 30 = 76.9% of maximal velocity.) Tj
T*
(Calculation 2 \(Severe Hypoglycemia, [S] = 1.0 mM\):) Tj
T*
(V_hypoglycemia = \(30 * 1.0\) / \(1.5 + 1.0\) = 30 / 2.5 = 12.00 micromol/min/10^8 cells.) Tj
T*
(Operating saturation drops to 40% of Vmax, explaining clinical neurological symptoms) Tj
T*
(\(neuroglycopenia\) due to glucose depletion in brain erythrocytes.) Tj
T*
(Calculation 3 \(Substrate Specificity for D-galactose, [S] = 5.0 mM, Km = 30 mM\):) Tj
T*
(V_galactose = \(30 * 5.0\) / \(30 + 5.0\) = 150 / 35 = 4.29 micromol/min/10^8 cells.) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: GLUT1 shows a 5.4-fold higher transport rate for D-glucose over D-galactose) Tj
T*
(at identical substrate concentrations due to lower Km \(higher binding affinity\).) Tj
T*
